{"id":"7cd97bf2-169b-4aaa-8331-047d5b6809a0","object":"chat.completion","created":1784804931,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    round_scores = payload['round_scores']\\n    round_targets = payload['round_targets']\\n    forced = payload['forced']\\n    N = len(round_scores[0])\\n    if not all(len(scores) == N for scores in round_scores):\\n        raise ValueError('Inconsistent score lengths')\\n    if len(round_targets) != len(round_scores):\\n        raise ValueError('Mismatch targets and rounds')\\n    if any(t < 0 for t in round_targets):\\n        raise ValueError('Negative target')\\n    if not all(0 <= f < N for f in forced):\\n        raise ValueError('Forced index out of range')\\n    active = list(range(N))\\n    per_round_masks = []\\n    for i in range(len(round_scores)):\\n        target = round_targets[i]\\n        if target < len(forced):\\n            raise ValueError('Target less than forced count')\\n        if target > len(active):\\n            raise ValueError('Target exceeds active count')\\n        forced_active = [f for f in forced if f in active]\\n        non_forced = [idx for idx in active if idx not in forced_active]\\n        non_forced_sorted = sorted(non_forced, key=lambda idx: (-round_scores[i][idx], idx))\\n        pick = target - len(forced_active)\\n        if pick < 0 or pick > len(non_forced):\\n            raise ValueError('Cannot pick enough non-forced')\\n        selected_non_forced = set(non_forced_sorted[:pick])\\n        kept = set(forced_active) | selected_non_forced\\n        mask = [j in kept for j in range(N)]\\n        per_round_masks.append(mask)\\n        active = sorted(kept)\\n    final_retained_indices = active\\n    return {'per_round_masks': per_round_masks, 'final_retained_indices': final_retained_indices}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that does iterative token compression. Let's parse the problem: We have multiple rounds (each round has scores and a target number of tokens to keep). There are forced indices (list of indices that must be kept). The process: start with all original token indices active. For each round, rank only currently active indices by descending supplied score (score for that round). Then we need to keep forced indices, then fill remaining positions up to target with top-ranked among remaining active indices (excluding forced). But must preserve original order in every retained mask. Also reject if target is infeasible (e.g., target less than number of forced indices? Or target greater than number of active indices? Let's define constraints.)\n\nFrom examples: round_scores: list of lists, one per round. round_targets: list of integers, one per round. forced: list of indices that must be kept in the final selection. The process is iterative: after each round, we only keep the selected indices. So we start with all indices 0..N-1 where N is length of first round scores? But scores per round have same length? In fixtures, round_scores has two lists each of length 8. So N=8. forced indices are in range 0..7. round_targets: [6,4] meaning first round keep 6 tokens, second round keep 4 tokens. forced: [4] means index 4 is forced to be kept throughout.\n\nLet's think: begin with active set = all indices. For each round i: we have scores_i for all indices? But scores are only for original indices? Actually each round_scores[i] is a list of scores for each original index. So we have score for each original index for each round. So we can use the scores of the current round to rank active indices. But careful: In each round, we need to select target_i tokens from the currently active set. We must keep forced indices (those in forced list) if they are still active. Also we must preserve original order in retained mask (i.e., when we output mask, the order of indices should be increasing). The output should include per-round masks (list of boolean masks for each round indicating which original indices are kept after that round) and final retained indices (list of indices that survived all rounds). Also need to raise ValueError if infeasible.\n\nInfeasibility: if any round's target is less than the number of forced indices that are still active? But forced indices are global, they must be kept in every round. So if target < len(forced), infeasible. Also if target > number of active indices, infeasible. Also if forced indices include invalid indices (outside range). Also check that round_targets list length matches number of rounds (len(round_scores)).\n\nAlso note: forced indices might be fewer than target. We need to select additional top-scoring indices (from remaining active indices) to fill up to target, preserving original order. The ranking is by descending score for that round. But after selecting forced, we fill remaining positions with top-scoring remaining active indices. Since we preserve original order, we need to output the mask in original order, not sorted by score. So we select the set of indices to keep (forced + top-scoring others), then output a boolean mask for that round indicating which original indices are kept (True). And then the active set for next round is exactly those kept indices (intersection of original order). The final retained indices list is the kept indices after last round, in original order.\n\nThe procedure: For each round i: \n- Get current active set (list of indices in increasing order)\n- Ensure target_i is feasible: target_i >= number of forced indices that are in active set? Actually forced indices are always considered active? They are in active set initially and remain if kept. But if a forced index is not in active set because it was not kept in a previous round? That can't happen because forced must be kept always. So we must ensure we never drop forced indices. So we enforce that in each round, forced indices are always selected. So we compute: forced_in_active = [f for f in forced if f in active_set]. (Initially all forced are in active_set). If len(forced_in_active) > target_i -> infeasible.\n- Also if target_i > len(active_set) -> infeasible (cannot keep more than are active).\n- Then we need to select additional indices from active_set that are not forced. We need to sort active_set by descending score_i[original_index] but we need to pick top (target_i - len(forced_in_active)) among non-forced active indices. But we need to preserve original order in output mask. So we collect the set of indices to keep = forced_in_active + top scoring non-forced indices (in order of score descending, but if tie? Use original index order? The problem says: \"All ranking and traversal ties use the source position or identifier specified above; never depend on hash-map iteration.\" So for ties in score, use lower index first? But we need to decide tie-breaking for ranking. Since we are selecting top scores, we need a stable sort that respects original index when scores equal. But scores are floats, unlikely equal. We can sort by (-score, index). Then take first K. Then the resulting set of indices is sorted by original index to produce mask.\n\nBut careful: The selection of top scores among non-forced active indices must be done based on the scores of the current round. We need to ensure we do not consider forced indices in the ranking (they are already selected). So we filter forced_out = [i for i in active_set if i not in forced_in_active]. Then sort these by (-score_i[i], i). Then take the first (target_i - len(forced_in_active)) indices. Combine with forced_in_active to get kept_set. Then output mask for this round: boolean list of length N (original number of tokens) where mask[i] = True if i in kept_set. Then active_set for next round = kept_set sorted by original index (i.e., increasing order). Continue.\n\nAfter all rounds, final retained indices = active_set after last round (sorted).\n\nReturn dict: per_round_masks (list of lists of bools, each length N), final_retained_indices (list of ints). Also we might need to include something else? The description says \"Return the per-round masks and the final retained indices.\" That seems sufficient.\n\nExample: For case 000: round_scores two lists length 8, round_targets [6,4], forced [4]. Start active = [0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]. Round 0 target 6. forced_in_active = [4] size 1, so need 5 more. Scores round 0: [0.876,0.564,0.398,0.867,0.955,0.861,0.147,0.827]. Sort non-forced active indices by descending score: index4 is forced so exclude. The rest: scores: 0:0.876,1:0.564,2:0.398,3:0.867,5:0.861,6:0.147,7:0.827. Sorted descending: 0 (0.876), 7 (0.827), 3 (0.867?), wait 0.876 > 0.867? Actually 0.876 > 0.867, so order: 0, 3, 5? 0.861, 7? 0.827 < 0.861? Let's compute: 0:0.876, 3:0.867, 5:0.861, 7:0.827, then 1:0.564, 2:0.398, 6:0.147. So top 5 non-forced: 0,3,5,7,1? Wait need 5, so take indices 0,3,5,7,1? But 1 has score 0.564. That gives kept_set = forced 4 + {0,3,5,7,1}. Sorted: 0,1,3,4,5,7. So mask for round 0: indices 0,1,3,4,5,7 = True. Then round 1: active = [0,1,3,4,5,7]. target=4. forced_in_active = [4] (still). Need 3 more. Scores round1: for indices: 0:0.664986,1:0.360359,3:0.01113057,4:0.18849451,5:0.17335601,7:0.30451683? Actually second list: [0.66498626,0.36035978,0.30007847,0.01113057,0.18849451,0.17335601,0.31970905,0.30451683]. So for active indices: 0:0.664986,1:0.360359,3:0.01113,4:0.188494,5:0.173356,7:0.304517. Sort non-forced (exclude 4): 0,1,7,5,3? Actually scores descending: 0>1>7>5>3. Need 3, so take 0,1,7. Kept set = forced 4 + {0,1,7} => [0,1,4,7]. Final retained indices = [0,1,4,7]. Let's verify with expected? Not given, but seems plausible.\n\nSo implement accordingly.\n\nNow code: define solve(case). Extract payload. Validate required fields: round_scores, round_targets, forced exist. Also check that round_scores is list of lists, all same length N, and round_targets length matches number of rounds, and forced is list of ints within 0..N-1. Check infeasibility cases: if any round target < len(forced) or > len(active) (active size decreases) etc. Also if forced indices duplicate? Should be fine. Also if forced index not in original range? Raise ValueError.\n\nWe must not mutate case. No random, no network, etc.\n\nReturn a dict with keys: per_round_masks, final_retained_indices. Actually per_round_masks: list of lists of bool for each round. final_retained_indices: list of ints.\n\nWe need to ensure JSON serializable: bool is fine, int is fine.\n\nEdge: N = len(round_scores[0]). All scores lists must have same length.\n\nImplementation in Python. Use strict typing? Not required.\n\nWe'll write solve(case) that returns dict.\n\nNow produce JSON with key implementation, value is Python source string escaped.\n\nWe must output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". So final output: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nWe need to escape backslashes, quotes, newlines. Use json.dumps on the string.\n\nBe careful: ensure that the source code is valid Python and does not contain any illegal characters. The code must be within a string in JSON.\n\nI'll write the code as a multi-line string, then use json.dumps to escape it. But we need to embed that in a JSON object. So we'll construct the entire JSON manually or use json.dumps on a dict.\n\nLet's write the Python source as a string with proper indentation. Then we'll output the JSON object.\n\nBecause we must ensure no trailing spaces, etc. But it's fine.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    payload = case['payload']\\n    scores = payload['round_scores']\\n    targets = payload['round_targets']\\n    forced = payload['forced']\\n    ...\"}\n\nI need to be careful with double quotes inside the source. If source contains double quotes, we need to escape them with backslash. But we can use single quotes in Python to avoid escaping.\n\nWe'll write code using single quotes for strings. That avoids escaping double quotes in JSON string. But we need to escape backslashes and newlines.\n\nBetter to use json.dumps to handle escaping.\n\nI'll construct a dict {\"implementation\": code_string} then dumps it.\n\nBut to avoid newlines in the code_string within the JSON, we need to ensure code_string has actual newline characters, which json.dumps will escape as \\n.\n\nLet's do it step by step in our head, but we need to output final.\n\nI'll write the code now.\n\ndef solve(case):\n    payload = case['payload']\n    round_scores = payload['round_scores']\n    round_targets = payload['round_targets']\n    forced = payload['forced']\n    N = len(round_scores[0])\n    # validate\n    if not all(len(scores) == N for scores in round_scores):\n        raise ValueError(\"Inconsistent score lengths\")\n    if len(round_targets) != len(round_scores):\n        raise ValueError(\"Mismatch targets and rounds\")\n    if any(t < 0 for t in round_targets):\n        raise ValueError(\"Negative target\")\n    if not all(0 <= f < N for f in forced):\n        raise ValueError(\"Forced index out of range\")\n    # also check duplicates? Not necessary.\n    active = list(range(N))\n    per_round_masks = []\n    for i in range(len(round_scores)):\n        target = round_targets[i]\n        if target < len(forced):\n            raise ValueError(\"Target less than forced count\")\n        if target > len(active):\n            raise ValueError(\"Target exceeds active count\")\n        # forced indices that are still active (should be all)\n        forced_active = [f for f in forced if f in active]\n        # remaining non-forced active indices\n        non_forced = [idx for idx in active if idx not in forced_active]\n        # sort non-forced by descending score of round i, then by index ascending for ties\n        # scores[i][idx]\n        non_forced_sorted = sorted(non_forced, key=lambda idx: (-round_scores[i][idx], idx))\n        # number to pick from non-forced\n        pick = target - len(forced_active)\n        if pick < 0 or pick > len(non_forced):\n            raise ValueError(\"Cannot pick enough non-forced\")\n        selected_non_forced = set(non_forced_sorted[:pick])\n        kept = set(forced_active) | selected_non_forced\n        # produce mask in original order\n        mask = [j in kept for j in range(N)]\n        per_round_masks.append(mask)\n        # update active for next round: keep only those in kept, sorted\n        active = sorted(kept)\n    # after all rounds\n    final_retained_indices = active\n    return {\n        'per_round_masks': per_round_masks,\n        'final_retained_indices': final_retained_indices\n    }\n\nThat should work. But we need to ensure we don't use any external libs. This is fine.\n\nEdge: forced might have duplicates? We can treat set but our forced_active list comprehension will include duplicates? Actually if forced has duplicates, then forced_active will have duplicates. That might cause issues. We should treat forced as a set to avoid duplicates. But problem says forced list. We can make forced = list(set(forced))? But better to validate no duplicates? The statement: \"forced: list of indices that must be kept\". Typically no duplicates. But we can handle by converting to set to avoid counting duplicates twice. However, if there is a duplicate, then len(forced) would be wrong. We'll assume valid input. For safety, we can convert forced to set early.\n\nAlso note: forced indices may not be in active because they were dropped earlier? But we never drop forced, so they remain active. But if forced index was not in active originally? That can't happen because active starts with all indices. So it's fine.\n\nAnother nuance: The scores per round are given for all original indices. When we update active, we only consider those indices. So sorting non_forced based on scores of those indices is fine.\n\nNow produce JSON. We'll have a string that i